description:
FOUND by s538b (#2371, s540) reading Try-Tiny t/finally.t rows 17 18 23 (newly reached once ScopeGuard DESTROY runs at exit).  PRE-EXISTING on main 53dd73da (probed on the extraction).  SILENT WRONG.

In a MODULE (a file loaded by use/require, compiled through pl2cl --module), `local $h{K} = V` on a FILE-LEXICAL hash (`my %h;` at file level) does not install V where the rest of the sub reads it.  Program mode (the same text as the main script) is right; the emission is IDENTICAL in both modes (`(p-local-hash-elem-init %h "q" 9 ...)` / `(p-gethash %h "q")`), so the difference is in how the compiled module resolves %h.  `our %o` in the module is right.

Reproducer (mm3/MM3.pm + g11.pl in ~/pcl/.claude/worktrees/agent-a20d76d2098a008d9/scratch/s538b/probes/r540/):
  # MM3.pm
  package MM3;
  our %o; my %h;
  sub f3 { local $o{q} = 7; print "our=", $o{q}, "\n"; }
  sub f4 { local $h{q}; $h{q} = 8; print "my-then-assign=", $h{q}, "\n"; }
  sub f5 { local $h{q} = 9; print "my-init=", $h{q}, "\n"; }
  1;
  # g11.pl
  use lib "DIR/mm3"; use MM3; MM3::f3(); MM3::f4(); MM3::f5();
perl 5.40.3:  our=7 / my-then-assign=8 / my-init=9
PCL (main):   our=7 / my-then-assign=8 / my-init=8   <- f4's value, which its local should have removed
Also: `local $h{q} = 7; print $h{q}` prints EMPTY; `local $h{g} = [1,2]; scalar(@{$h{g}})` is 0 (under strict: dies "Can't use an undefined value as an ARRAY reference").  PCL_NO_FASL_CACHE=1 + a fresh PCL_CACHE_DIR: same.

Reading: the two local macros (p-local-hash-elem / -init: `(unbox %h)`) and the element accessors (p-gethash / p-setf p-gethash) reach DIFFERENT hash objects for a module's file lexical -- f4's restore deletes the key from one while its assignment wrote the other.  Look at how a module's file-lexical cell (p-defcell at module top level) and the p-sub `:captures (%h)` declaration are compiled (compile-file: a cell value dumped as a literal?).

Effect on the board: Try::Tiny (real dist, not PCL's lib/Try/Tiny.pm shim) keeps its finally guards in `local $_finally_guards{guards} = [...]` on a file-lexical hash; the error is never pushed onto the guards, so every finally block gets an EMPTY @_ (finally.t rows 17 18 23).  Any CPAN module using local on a file-lexical hash element is affected.

